# Port Security Issue and Troubleshooting

Because I completed this lab over a couple days, I encountered an issue when configuring port security.

The `switchport port-security` command was being rejected. After troubleshooting, I discovered that the interfaces were configured as dynamic switchports instead of static access ports. I did not remember configuring them this way because I believed I had previously configured the ports as access ports.

I intended for ports Gi0/1 through Gi0/48 to be access ports, with the exception of the TenGigabit interfaces used for other connections.

To correct the issue, I configured the interfaces as access ports before enabling port security:

enable
conf t
interface range Gi0/1 - 48
switchport mode access
switchport port-security
switchport port-security maximum 1
switchport port-security mac-address


This was a good troubleshooting experience because it reinforced the importance of verifying the current interface configuration instead of assuming it was configured correctly.



# Unused Ports

As a security best practice, I shut down unused switch ports.

Because the switch is not currently connected to any devices, I chose to shut down ports 25 through 48. I also assigned the unused ports to VLAN 999, which I created as a separate unused VLAN.

This helps prevent unauthorized devices from connecting to unused switch ports.



# What I Learned

This lab gave me additional practice configuring a Cisco switch from scratch. I reviewed basic switch configuration, SSH, VLANs, access ports, trunk ports, port security, and disabling unused ports.

One of the most valuable parts of the lab was troubleshooting the port security issue. The experience reinforced the importance of checking the current switch configuration when a command does not work as expected.


